<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="ma310-written-hw01">
  <title>MA-310 Calculus III: Written Homework 1</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Prepare neatly written solutions to the following problems.
      Show all relevant work and use proper notation and terminology.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <!-- ==================== QUESTION 1 ==================== -->

  <page xml:id="page-sphere">
    <title>Problem 1</title>

    <exercise xml:id="sphere-center-radius">
      <statement>
        <p> Find the center and radius of the sphere given by  </p>

        <me>  x^2+y^2+z^2-10x+3y+5z+\frac{47}{2}=0. </me>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>  Group the terms involving each variable: </p>

        <me>
          (x^2-10x)+(y^2+3y)+(z^2+5z) =-\frac{47}{2}.
        </me>

        <p> Complete the square for each variable: </p>

        <me>  x^2-10x=(x-5)^2-25. </me>

        <me>
          y^2+3y=\left(y+\frac{3}{2}\right)^2-\frac{9}{4}.
        </me>

        <me>
          z^2+5z=\left(z+\frac{5}{2}\right)^2-\frac{25}{4}.
        </me>

        <p> Substitute these expressions into the equation: </p>

        <me>
          (x-5)^2+
          \left(y+\frac{3}{2}\right)^2+
          \left(z+\frac{5}{2}\right)^2
          =
          -\frac{47}{2}+25+\frac{9}{4}+\frac{25}{4}.
        </me>

        <p>  Simplify the right-hand side: </p>

        <me>
          -\frac{47}{2}+25+\frac{9}{4}+\frac{25}{4}=10.
        </me>

        <p> Therefore, the sphere is in standard form: </p>

        <me>
          (x-5)^2+
          \left(y+\frac{3}{2}\right)^2+
          \left(z+\frac{5}{2}\right)^2
          =10.
        </me>

        <p> The standard equation of a sphere is </p>

        <me>  (x-h)^2+(y-k)^2+(z-\ell)^2=r^2. </me>

        <p> Thus, the center is </p>

        <me>  \left(5,-\frac{3}{2},-\frac{5}{2}\right) </me>

        <p>  and the radius is </p>

        <me>  \sqrt{10}. </me>

        <p>
          Therefore,
          <em>Center:</em>
          <m>\left(5,-\frac{3}{2},-\frac{5}{2}\right)</m>
          and
          <em>radius:</em>
          <m>\sqrt{10}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </page>


  <!-- ==================== QUESTION 2 ==================== -->

  <page xml:id="page-vectors">
    <title>Problem 2</title>

    <exercise xml:id="vector-computations">
      <statement>
        <p>  Let </p>

        <me>
          \mathbf{a}=3\mathbf{i}-\mathbf{j}-4\mathbf{k}
        </me>

        <p>  and </p>

        <me>
          \mathbf{b}=2\mathbf{i}+5\mathbf{j}-2\mathbf{k}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Find each of the following:
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              a unit vector having the opposite direction of
              <m>\mathbf{a}</m>;
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              the vector <m>3\mathbf{a}-5\mathbf{b}</m>;
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              the angle between the vectors
              <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>


      <solution>
        <ol>

          <!-- PART A -->

          <li>
            <p>
              First find the magnitude of <m>\mathbf{a}</m>:
            </p>

            <me>
              \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert
              =
              \sqrt{3^2+(-1)^2+(-4)^2}
              =
              \sqrt{26}.
            </me>

            <p>
              A unit vector in the direction of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> is
            </p>

            <me>
              \frac{\mathbf{a}}{\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert}
              =
              \frac{1}{\sqrt{26}}
              \langle 3,-1,-4\rangle.
            </me>

            <p>
              Therefore, a unit vector having the opposite direction
              of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> is
            </p>

            <me>
              -\frac{\mathbf{a}}{\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert}
              =
              \left\langle
              -\frac{3}{\sqrt{26}},
              \frac{1}{\sqrt{26}},
              \frac{4}{\sqrt{26}}
              \right\rangle.
            </me>

            <p>
              Hence the answer is
            </p>

            <me>
              \boxed{
              \left\langle
              -\frac{3}{\sqrt{26}},
              \frac{1}{\sqrt{26}},
              \frac{4}{\sqrt{26}}
              \right\rangle}.
            </me>
          </li>


          <!-- PART B -->

          <li>
            <p>
              Write the vectors in component form:
            </p>

            <me>
              \mathbf{a}=\langle3,-1,-4\rangle,
              \qquad
              \mathbf{b}=\langle2,5,-2\rangle.
            </me>

            <p>
              Calculate <m>3\mathbf{a}</m>:
            </p>

            <me>
              3\mathbf{a}
              =
              3\langle3,-1,-4\rangle
              =
              \langle9,-3,-12\rangle.
            </me>

            <p>
              Calculate <m>5\mathbf{b}</m>:
            </p>

            <me>
              5\mathbf{b}
              =
              5\langle2,5,-2\rangle
              =
              \langle10,25,-10\rangle.
            </me>

            <p>
              Therefore,
            </p>

            <me>
              3\mathbf{a}-5\mathbf{b}
              =
              \langle 9,-3,-12\rangle
              -
              \langle 10,25,-10\rangle
            </me>

            <me>
              =\langle -1,-28,-2\rangle
            </me>

            <p>
              Hence,
            </p>

            <me>
              \boxed{3\mathbf{a}-5\mathbf{b}
              =\langle-1,-28,-2\rangle}.
            </me>
          </li>


          <!-- PART C -->

          <li>
            <p>
              Use the dot-product formula
            </p>

            <me>
              \mathbf{a}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathbf{b}
              =
              \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert
              \lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert
              \cos(\theta).
            </me>

            <p>
              First calculate the dot product:
            </p>

            <me>
              \mathbf{a}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathbf{b}
              =(3)(2)+(-1)(5)+(-4)(-2)
              =6-5+8
              =9.
            </me>

            <p>
              We already know that
            </p>

            <me>
              \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert=\sqrt{26}.
            </me>

            <p>
              Also,
            </p>

            <me>
              \lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert
              =
              \sqrt{2^2+5^2+(-2)^2}
              =
              \sqrt{33}.
            </me>

            <p>
              Substitute these values into the dot-product formula:
            </p>

            <me>
              9=\sqrt{26}\sqrt{33}\cos(\theta).
            </me>

            <p>
              Since
              <m>\sqrt{26}\sqrt{33}=\sqrt{858}</m>,
              we have
            </p>

            <me>
              \cos(\theta)=\frac{9}{\sqrt{858}}.
            </me>

            <p>
              Therefore,
            </p>

            <me>
              \theta=
              \cos^{-1}\left(\frac{9}{\sqrt{858}}\right).
            </me>

            <p>
              In degrees,
            </p>

            <me>
              \theta\approx72.1^\circ.
            </me>

            <p>
              Hence,
            </p>

            <me>
              \boxed{
              \theta=
              \cos^{-1}\left(\frac{9}{\sqrt{858}}\right)
              \approx72.1^\circ}.
            </me>
          </li>

        </ol>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </page>

</worksheet>
